\begin{abstract}
We develop a self-contained algebraic and probabilistic framework for security reductions involving multilinear Diffie--Hellman challenges and graded encoding systems. A graded system over a commutative monoid has level-indexed encoding spaces, canonical encoding maps, and a multiplication that adds levels while representing plaintext multiplication. This interface yields canonical multilinear evaluation and an exact transcript representation of a $k$-ary product challenge. A finite decisional game is modeled by two transcript mass functions; the advantage of a deterministic Boolean distinguisher is the absolute difference of its acceptance probabilities. A perfect reduction is a bijection of transcript spaces preserving mass pointwise in both challenge worlds. We prove that such a reduction preserves each acceptance probability and hence distinguishing advantage exactly. Source hardness therefore transfers without loss, while every strict target attack induces an equally strong source attack. For imperfect simulations on a common finite transcript space, target advantage is at most source advantage plus the sum of the $\ell^1$ simulation gaps in the random and real worlds. Algorithms and numerical examples illustrate both forms of transfer.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Graded encoding systems support controlled algebra on hidden values. An encoding carries a concealed plaintext and a public level. Multiplication combines plaintexts while adding levels, so $k$ level-one inputs can produce a level-$k$ encoding of their product. This mechanism abstracts a capability sought in multilinear cryptography: independently supplied inputs participate in a joint computation without exposing unrestricted algebraic access.

Algebraic capability alone does not establish security. A reduction must connect a target graded transcript to a source problem believed hard, such as decisional multilinear Diffie--Hellman. It must specify the encoded target, transform a target distinguisher into a source distinguisher, and quantify any loss in advantage.

We separate these concerns into two layers. The algebraic layer uses only a commutative monoid, level-indexed codes, canonical encodings, graded multiplication, and compatibility with plaintext multiplication. The probabilistic layer treats finite two-world games. Under a mass-preserving transcript bijection, acceptance is preserved world by world and advantage is preserved exactly. Under approximate simulation, a triangle inequality bounds the loss by two worldwise $\ell^1$ errors.

The contribution is foundational rather than candidate-specific. It does not assert that a particular construction satisfies a multilinear Diffie--Hellman assumption. It states what follows once a construction supplies the algebraic interface and the perfect or approximate simulation hypotheses.

\section{Algebraic framework}

\subsection{Commutative plaintext multiplication}

Let $R$ be a commutative monoid with multiplication and identity $1$. Thus $(xy)z=x(yz)$, $xy=yx$, and $1x=x1=x$. Inverses are not needed.

\begin{definition}[Graded encoding system]
A graded encoding system over $R$ consists of a set $C_i$ for each $i\in\mathbb N$, a canonical map $E_i:R\to C_i$, and multiplication maps
\[
\odot_{i,j}:C_i\times C_j\longrightarrow C_{i+j}
\]
such that, for every $x,y\in R$,
\[
E_i(x)\odot_{i,j}E_j(y)=E_{i+j}(xy).
\]
\end{definition}

The level is structural: level $i$ multiplied by level $j$ lands at level $i+j$. The compatibility law concerns canonical encodings; no assumptions about equality testing, noise, rerandomization, or noncanonical representatives are required.

\begin{definition}[Canonical multilinear evaluation]
For a finite list $\mathbf{x}=(x_1,\ldots,x_n)$ in $R$, set
\[
\Eval(\mathbf{x})=E_n\!\left(\prod_{r=1}^{n}x_r\right)\in C_n.
\]
For the empty list the product is $1$, so the value is $E_0(1)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Incremental multilinear evaluation]
For every finite list $\mathbf{x}=(x_1,\ldots,x_n)$ and every $x\in R$,
\[
\Eval(\mathbf{x})\odot E_1(x)
=E_{n+1}\!\left(\left(\prod_{r=1}^{n}x_r\right)x\right).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The first factor is the canonical level-$n$ encoding of $\prod_r x_r$. Apply the compatibility law at levels $n$ and $1$ to that plaintext and $x$. The output level is $n+1$ and its plaintext is the stated product.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Multilinear Diffie--Hellman challenges}

Fix $k\in\mathbb N$. A source challenge is a tuple $\mathbf a=(a_1,\ldots,a_k)\in R^k$, with multilinear target
\[
T(\mathbf a)=\prod_{i=1}^{k}a_i.
\]

\begin{definition}[Canonical graded challenge transcript]
The canonical transcript associated with $\mathbf a$ consists of the public level-one encodings $E_1(a_1),\ldots,E_1(a_k)$ and the level-$k$ target encoding $E_k(T(\mathbf a))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Canonical challenge-target identity]
The target component of the canonical transcript of $\mathbf a$ is
\[
E_k\!\left(\prod_{i=1}^{k}a_i\right).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Substitute the definition $T(\mathbf a)=\prod_i a_i$ into $E_k(T(\mathbf a))$.
\end{proof}

This elementary identity is the precise bridge between the source tuple and its graded transcript.

\section{Finite decisional games}

Let $\Omega$ be finite. A two-world game $G$ specifies mass functions $P^G_0,P^G_1:\Omega\to\mathbb R$, where $0$ denotes the random world and $1$ the real world. In probabilistic applications these masses are nonnegative and normalized, although the algebraic statements below use only finite sums and stated mass relations.

\begin{definition}[Acceptance]
For a deterministic Boolean distinguisher $A:\Omega\to\{0,1\}$, define
\[
\Acc^G_b(A)=\sum_{\omega\in\Omega:A(\omega)=1}P^G_b(\omega).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Advantage]
The absolute distinguishing advantage is
\[
\Adv^G(A)=\left|\Acc^G_1(A)-\Acc^G_0(A)\right|.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[World-swap invariance]
For every $G$ and $A$,
\[
|\Acc^G_0(A)-\Acc^G_1(A)|=\Adv^G(A).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Use $|u-v|=|v-u|$.
\end{proof}

This information-theoretic formulation can be restricted to an efficient class of distinguishers in computational applications.

\section{Perfect reductions}

Let $G_S$ be a source game on a finite set $S$ and $G_T$ a target game on a finite set $T$.

\begin{definition}[Perfect reduction]
A perfect reduction from $G_S$ to $G_T$ is a bijection $\phi:S\to T$ satisfying
\[
P^{G_T}_b(\phi(s))=P^{G_S}_b(s)
\]
for every $s\in S$ and $b\in\{0,1\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Reduced distinguisher]
Given $A:T\to\{0,1\}$, its source reduction is $A\circ\phi:S\to\{0,1\}$, with $(A\circ\phi)(s)=A(\phi(s))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Acceptance preservation]
For every target distinguisher $A$ and world $b$,
\[
\Acc^{G_S}_b(A\circ\phi)=\Acc^{G_T}_b(A).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Expand the source acceptance sum:
\[
\Acc^{G_S}_b(A\circ\phi)
=\sum_{s\in S:A(\phi(s))=1}P^{G_S}_b(s).
\]
Replace each source mass with $P^{G_T}_b(\phi(s))$. Since $\phi$ is bijective, reindex by $t=\phi(s)$. Every $t\in T$ appears exactly once and the acceptance condition becomes $A(t)=1$. The result is $\sum_{t:A(t)=1}P^{G_T}_b(t)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact advantage preservation]
For every target distinguisher $A$,
\[
\Adv^{G_S}(A\circ\phi)=\Adv^{G_T}(A).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Expand both advantages as absolute differences, then apply acceptance preservation in worlds $1$ and $0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Lossless hardness transfer]
If every source distinguisher $B:S\to\{0,1\}$ satisfies $\Adv^{G_S}(B)\leq\varepsilon$, then every target distinguisher $A:T\to\{0,1\}$ satisfies $\Adv^{G_T}(A)\leq\varepsilon$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Apply source hardness to $B=A\circ\phi$ and use exact advantage preservation.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Attack conversion]
If $\varepsilon<\Adv^{G_T}(A)$, then
\[
\varepsilon<\Adv^{G_S}(A\circ\phi).
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Substitute the equality of advantages.
\end{proof}

Thus a perfect reduction is lossless: a target attack becomes a source attack of precisely the same statistical strength.

\section{Approximate game hops}

Suppose now that $G_S$ and $G_T$ use the same finite transcript space $\Omega$.

\begin{definition}[$\ell^1$ gap]
For finite mass functions $P,Q:\Omega\to\mathbb R$, define
\[
d_1(P,Q)=\sum_{\omega\in\Omega}|P(\omega)-Q(\omega)|.
\]
\end{definition}

For any Boolean acceptance set,
\[
\left|\sum_{A(\omega)=1}P(\omega)-\sum_{A(\omega)=1}Q(\omega)\right|
\leq d_1(P,Q)
\]
by the triangle inequality.

\begin{theorem}[Approximate game-hop bound]
Assume
\[
d_1(P^{G_T}_0,P^{G_S}_0)\leq\delta_0,
\qquad
d_1(P^{G_T}_1,P^{G_S}_1)\leq\delta_1.
\]
Then every deterministic Boolean distinguisher $A$ satisfies
\[
\Adv^{G_T}(A)
\leq\Adv^{G_S}(A)+\delta_0+\delta_1.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Insert the source acceptance probabilities into the target difference:
\begin{align*}
\Acc^{G_T}_1(A)-\Acc^{G_T}_0(A)
={}&(\Acc^{G_S}_1(A)-\Acc^{G_S}_0(A))\\
&+(\Acc^{G_T}_1(A)-\Acc^{G_S}_1(A))\\
&+(\Acc^{G_S}_0(A)-\Acc^{G_T}_0(A)).
\end{align*}
Apply the triangle inequality. The first absolute term is source advantage. The other two terms are bounded by the corresponding $\ell^1$ gaps, and hence by $\delta_1$ and $\delta_0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Approximate hardness transfer]
If every $A$ satisfies $\Adv^{G_S}(A)\leq\varepsilon$ and the preceding world-gap assumptions hold, then
\[
\Adv^{G_T}(A)\leq\varepsilon+\delta_0+\delta_1
\]
for every $A$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Combine the approximate game-hop theorem with the source bound.
\end{proof}

The bound separates intrinsic source advantage from random-world and real-world simulation errors. Perfect transfer is recovered when both errors vanish.

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Canonical evaluation}

To evaluate plaintexts $x_1,\ldots,x_n$, initialize $p\leftarrow1$, update $p\leftarrow px_i$ for each input, and return $E_n(p)$. This requires $O(n)$ monoid operations and one encoding. Equivalently, one may multiply canonical level-one encodings incrementally; the incremental evaluation theorem supplies the invariant after every step.

\subsection{Distinguisher reduction}

On source transcript $s$, compute $t\leftarrow\phi(s)$, run $A(t)$, and return its bit. Runtime is the cost of $\phi$ plus that of $A$, with constant composition overhead. An information-theoretic perfect reduction therefore becomes a computational reduction when $\phi$ is efficiently computable.

\subsection{Finite reduction audit}

For explicitly tabulated games: verify bijectivity of $\phi$; compare $P^{G_S}_b(s)$ with $P^{G_T}_b(\phi(s))$ for every $s$ and $b$; compute source and target acceptance; and, if equality fails, compute both worldwise $\ell^1$ gaps. For $N$ transcripts, each pass takes $O(N)$ time and the distribution tables use $O(N)$ storage.

\section{Numerical examples}

\begin{example}[Exact preservation]
Let
\[
P^{G_S}_0=(0.40,0.30,0.20,0.10),\qquad
P^{G_S}_1=(0.10,0.20,0.30,0.40).
\]
Relabel the four transcripts by any permutation and transport each mass along that permutation. If a target distinguisher accepts the images of the last two source points, its random acceptance is $0.20+0.10=0.30$, its real acceptance is $0.30+0.40=0.70$, and its advantage is $0.40$. The reduced source distinguisher accepts those same two source points and has identical values.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Approximate transfer]
On the same ordered space, let
\[
P^{G_T}_0=(0.39,0.31,0.21,0.09),\qquad
P^{G_T}_1=(0.12,0.19,0.29,0.40).
\]
The two $\ell^1$ gaps are $0.04$ and $0.04$. Hence every Boolean distinguisher obeys
\[
\Adv^{G_T}(A)\leq\Adv^{G_S}(A)+0.08.
\]
If source advantage is universally at most $0.05$, target advantage is at most $0.13$.
\end{example}

\section{Applications and limitations}

The framework supports the standard multilinear reduction narrative. A source experiment samples hidden factors and forms either a genuine product target or a random comparison target. A target construction exposes a graded transcript. A mass-preserving bijection proves that representational translation has no security cost; approximate world bounds quantify imperfect simulation.

Potential applications include multipartite key exchange, hybrid arguments, comparisons of equivalent transcript formats, and explicit security accounting. In multipartite settings the algebraic theorem tracks the product and total level, while the probabilistic theorem transfers a separate decisional hardness assumption. In hybrid proofs the approximate theorem controls one hop and suggests summing adjacent losses.

The hypotheses also delimit the conclusions. Many reductions are randomized, many-to-one, or include aborts; these require Markov kernels, pushforward distributions, or subprobabilities. The distinguishers here are deterministic. No concrete candidate is made secure merely by satisfying graded multiplication: its actual challenge distributions must satisfy the required reduction hypotheses.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The exact and approximate results share one principle: advantage depends only on acceptance mass in two worlds. A perfect reduction preserves that mass by reindexing; an approximate hop controls its movement by a norm bound. Finite spaces keep the argument elementary, and a commutative monoid keeps the algebraic assumptions minimal.

For normalized distributions, the event-probability discrepancy is bounded by total variation distance, one half of full $\ell^1$ distance. This suggests the sharper bound
\[
\Adv^{G_T}(A)\leq\Adv^{G_S}(A)+\tfrac12\delta_0+\tfrac12\delta_1.
\]
Its proof uses cancellation of signed mass differences and therefore needs normalization.

Further directions are:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
\item lift exact preservation from Boolean distinguishers to randomized Markov kernels;
\item establish the half-$\ell^1$ bound for normalized distributions;
\item prove identity, composition, and functoriality laws for perfect reductions;
\item derive endpoint bounds for finite hybrid chains by summing adjacent world errors;
\item prove coherence of canonical graded evaluation for every binary parenthesization of level-one inputs.
\end{enumerate}

Computational costs should also be integrated explicitly. A reduction can preserve advantage exactly while imposing unacceptable runtime. Concrete constructions should instantiate the algebraic interface and derive mass preservation or quantitative gaps from their actual sampling algorithms.

\section{Conclusion}

A graded encoding system turns plaintext multiplication into level-raising encoded multiplication. Canonical multilinear evaluation and the challenge-target identity establish the intended algebra. Finite decision games then express security through acceptance mass and real-versus-random advantage.

A mass-preserving transcript bijection preserves acceptance in each world and therefore preserves every deterministic distinguisher's advantage exactly. Source hardness transfers without loss, while any target attack becomes an equally strong source attack. With approximate simulation, target advantage grows by at most the sum of the two worldwise $\ell^1$ errors. These results isolate the assumptions needed for tight multilinear Diffie--Hellman reductions and provide transparent accounting for both perfect and imperfect game transitions.

